{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0001","statement":"The paper proposes three regularization terms on top of generalized expectation criteria.","source_span":"we propose three regularization terms on top of generalized expectation criteria"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0001","statement":"The three regularization terms are: (1) one associated with neutral features, (2) the maximum entropy of class distribution term, and (3) the KL divergence between reference and predicted class distribution.","source_span":"we explore three regularization terms to address the problem: (1) a regularization term associated with neutral features; (2) the maximum entropy of class distribution regularization term; and (3) the KL divergence between reference and predicted class distribution"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0001","statement":"The paper addresses the robustness problem on top of GE-FL, a generalized expectation method that leverages labeled features as prior knowledge.","source_span":"We address the robustness problem on top of GE-FL BIBREF0 , a GE method which leverages labeled features as prior knowledge."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0001","statement":"GE-FL was proposed by Druck et al. to learn from labeled features using generalized expectation criteria.","source_span":"Druck et al. ge-fl proposed GE-FL to learn from labeled features using generalized expectation criteria."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0001","statement":"A labeled feature is a strong indicator of a specific class and is manually provided to the classifier.","source_span":"A labeled feature is a strong indicator of a specific class and is manually provided to the classifier."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0001","statement":"L-BFGS can be used for parameter estimation to solve the GE-FL optimization problem.","source_span":"To solve the optimization problem, L-BFGS can be used for parameter estimation."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0001","statement":"Supplying neutral feature words (words not strongly indicative of any class) can remarkably boost performance and robustness, without requiring manual annotation.","source_span":"supplying neutral feature words can boost the performance remarkably, making the model more robust... we do not need manual annotation to label these neutral feature words"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0001","fact_refs":[1],"question":"What three regularization terms does the paper propose to make generalized expectation criteria more robust?","answer":"The three terms are: (1) a term associated with neutral features, (2) a maximum entropy of class distribution term, and (3) the KL divergence between reference and predicted class distributions."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0001","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What existing method does this paper build on to address the robustness problem in text classification?","answer":"It builds on GE-FL, a generalized expectation method that leverages labeled features as prior knowledge."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0001","fact_refs":[3],"question":"Who originally proposed GE-FL and what is it used for?","answer":"GE-FL was proposed by Druck et al. to learn from labeled features using generalized expectation criteria."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0001","fact_refs":[4],"question":"How does this paper define a 'labeled feature'?","answer":"A labeled feature is a strong indicator of a specific class that is manually provided to the classifier (e.g., 'amazing' for the positive class)."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0001","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What optimization algorithm is used for parameter estimation in GE-FL?","answer":"L-BFGS is used for parameter estimation."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0001","fact_refs":[6],"question":"Does supplying neutral feature words in this approach require manual annotation, and what effect does it have?","answer":"No manual annotation is required to label neutral feature words, and supplying them remarkably boosts performance and makes the model more robust."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0002","statement":"The method is based on semi-automatic induction of a context-free grammar (CFG) from semantically annotated text.","source_span":"The method is based on semi-automatic induction of a context-free grammar from semantically annotated text."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0002","statement":"The approach was evaluated on the first sentences of Wikipedia pages describing people.","source_span":"The approach was evaluated on the first sentences of Wikipedia pages describing people."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0002","statement":"The grammar and semantic trees are used to learn the ontology on several levels: classes, instances, taxonomic and non-taxonomic relations.","source_span":"Both, the grammar and the semantic trees are used to learn the ontology on several levels -- classes, instances, taxonomic and non-taxonomic relations."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0002","statement":"The input to the joint learning process is a seed ontology together with text annotated with concepts from that seed ontology.","source_span":"The input is a seed ontology together with text annotated with concepts from the seed ontology."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0002","statement":"The grammar induction method follows curriculum learning, where the parser first learns to parse simple sentences, then proceeds to more complex ones.","source_span":"The grammar induction method works on the premise of curriculum learning BIBREF7 , where the parser first learns to parse simple sentences, then proceeds to learn more complex ones."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0002","statement":"The Wikipedia sentences used are already annotated with links to other pages that are also instances of the DBpedia knowledge base.","source_span":"These sentences are already annotated with links to other pages, which are also instances of DBpedia knowledge base BIBREF9 ."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0002","statement":"Relations from DBpedia are used as a training set to train and evaluate several models for predicting relations.","source_span":"Using relations from DBpedia as a training set, several models to predict relations have been trained and evaluated."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0002","statement":"The approach does not rely on any syntactic analysis of text, such as part-of-speech tagging or dependency parsing.","source_span":"Our approach does not rely on any syntactic analysis of text, like part-of-speech tagging or dependency parsing."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0002","statement":"One rule is added to the grammar on each iteration of the induction process.","source_span":"One rule is added to the grammar on each iteration."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0002","fact_refs":[1],"question":"What dataset was used to evaluate this joint ontology and semantic parser learning approach?","answer":"The approach was evaluated on the first sentences of Wikipedia pages describing people."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0002","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What is required as input to the grammar and ontology induction process?","answer":"The input is a seed ontology together with text annotated with concepts from that seed ontology."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0002","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What learning principle underlies the grammar induction method in this paper?","answer":"It follows curriculum learning: the parser first learns to parse simple sentences, then progresses to more complex ones."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0002","fact_refs":[5,6],"question":"What knowledge base provides the relations used to train the relation prediction models in this work?","answer":"DBpedia — the Wikipedia sentences are annotated with links to DBpedia instances, and DBpedia relations are used as the training set for relation prediction models."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0002","fact_refs":[7],"question":"Does the proposed approach rely on part-of-speech tagging or dependency parsing?","answer":"No, the approach does not rely on any syntactic analysis of text, such as part-of-speech tagging or dependency parsing."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0002","fact_refs":[8],"question":"How many grammar rules are added per iteration during the grammar induction process?","answer":"One rule is added to the grammar on each iteration."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0003","statement":"The authors call their model a 'dynamic neural module network'.","source_span":"We call this model a dynamic neural module network."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0003","statement":"Training data consists of (world, question, answer) triples, and the approach requires no supervision of network layouts.","source_span":"Training data consists of (world, question, answer) triples: our approach requires no supervision of network layouts."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0003","statement":"The model achieves state-of-the-art performance on two question answering tasks: one with questions about natural images, and another with compositional questions about United States geography.","source_span":"We achieve state-of-the-art performance on two markedly different question answering tasks: one with questions about natural images, and another with more compositional questions about United States geography."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0003","statement":"The model has two jointly trained components: a collection of freely composable neural modules, and a network layout predictor that assembles modules into complete networks tailored to each question.","source_span":"Our model has two components, trained jointly: first, a collection of neural \u201cmodules\u201d that can be freely composed...; second, a network layout predictor that assembles modules into complete deep networks tailored to each question"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0003","statement":"Parameters for the modules are learned jointly with network-assembly parameters via reinforcement learning, using only (world, question, answer) triples as supervision.","source_span":"Parameters for these modules are learned jointly with network-assembly parameters via reinforcement learning, with only (world, question, answer) triples as supervision."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0003","statement":"The 'find' module maps image features and a lexical item (e.g., 'bird') to a distribution over pixels, functioning as an attention mechanism.","source_span":"This first step, a generic module called find, can be expressed as a fragment of a neural network that maps from image features and a lexical item (here bird) to a distribution over pixels."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0003","statement":"Andreas15NMN's prior approach uses hand-written rules to deterministically transform dependency trees into network layouts, restricted to simple structures for non-synthetic data.","source_span":"Andreas15NMN use hand-written rules to deterministically transform dependency trees into layouts, and are restricted to producing simple structures like the above for non-synthetic data."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0003","fact_refs":[0],"question":"What name do the authors give to the model proposed in this paper for question answering?","answer":"They call it a 'dynamic neural module network.'"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0003","fact_refs":[1],"question":"What kind of supervision is used to train this question answering model?","answer":"Only (world, question, answer) triples are used; the approach requires no supervision of network layouts."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0003","fact_refs":[2],"question":"On which two question answering tasks does the model achieve state-of-the-art performance?","answer":"One task involves questions about natural images, and the other involves compositional questions about United States geography."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0003","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What are the two jointly trained components of the dynamic neural module network model?","answer":"A collection of freely composable neural 'modules' and a network layout predictor that assembles modules into complete networks tailored to each question."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0003","fact_refs":[4],"question":"How are the parameters for the neural modules learned in this model?","answer":"They are learned jointly with network-assembly parameters via reinforcement learning, using only (world, question, answer) triples as supervision."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0003","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What does the 'find' module do in the dynamic neural module network?","answer":"It maps image features and a lexical item (such as 'bird') to a distribution over pixels, acting as an attention mechanism."}
